messages:
sam: quick heads-up — i'm updating the streaming + pagination snippets on the SDK docs. found three small issues: TS still uses `cursor`, Go retry lacks jitter, py example doesn't show cancellation.

ivy: Split into tiny PRs please (one per language + one migration note). easier to review and rollback.

diego: i'll take Go. plan: add context cancellation + jittered backoff and a tiny test that bounds cumulative sleep so CI stays fast. example: `sleep = 100ms*(1<<i) + rand(0,50)ms`.

sam: i'll handle Python. want to show `with client.stream(...) as s:` pattern, how to read `resume_token`, and a short Troubleshooting FAQ about long prefix caches causing OOMs.

ivy: i'll do TS — will add both async-iterator and AbortController examples and include a short note about removing listeners for emitter-style usage. also update pagination link to the new spec: https://github.com/redwood-inference/specs/pagination

diego: Go draft snippet: ```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
defer cancel()
for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
    resp, err := client.Stream(ctx, req)
    if err == nil { break }
    sleep := time.Duration(100*(1<<i))*time.Millisecond + time.Duration(rand.Intn(50))*time.Millisecond
    time.Sleep(sleep)
}
```

rahul: small asks: 1) include jittered exponential backoff in all three languages (tiny random component) 2) add a compatibility note for older SDKs (v0.9 uses `cursor`, v1.x uses `resume_token`) 3) migration PR should be a short one-pager sandbox showing py/ts/go side-by-side.

sam: agreed. i'll include Rahul's backoff decorator for py and a note: "Prefer small jitter to reduce test flakiness."

ivy: TS examples (draft): ```ts
// async iterator
const controller = new AbortController();
try {
  for await (const chunk of client.streamResponses({signal: controller.signal})) {
    console.log(chunk.text);
  }
} catch (err) {
  controller.abort();
  throw err;
}
// emitter style (legacy)
const s = client.streamEmitter(req);
s.on('data', d => {});
s.on('error', e => { s.removeAllListeners(); });
```

diego: i'll add a unit test at `snippets/retry_jitter_test.go` asserting cumulative sleep < 1s for the test parameters. keeps CI fast while catching regressions.

sam: please open separate PRs: #7001 (py), #7002 (ts), #7003 (go), #7004 (migration + sandbox). tag docs-easy and add @ivy for editorial review.

docs-bot: (docs-bot) FYI: keep code-block lines < 120 chars; link-check will fail if old internal URLs remain.

ivy: quick nit — in migration note avoid deep internals (KV cache internals). keep high-level advice and link to infra doc for deep dives.

rahul: also add a tiny compatibility table in migration PR and reference the SDK changelog. helps users on older pinned versions.

sam: opened #7001 (py) and pushed jitter decorator + resume_token example. docs-bot flagged one line-length in a code block, i'll wrap.

diego: opened #7003 (go) with test and README in snippets dir. pinging @rahul for jitter range check.

ivy: opened #7002 (ts), added AbortController and async iterator. tagging @sam for quick doc pass.

docs-bot: (docs-bot) CI: #7001 link-check failed -> replaced old pagination link; lint warn: code-block wrap.

sam: fixed wrapping and updated external link in #7001. CI passing now. merging py first to unblock downstream migration note.

docs-bot: (docs-bot) #7001 merged.

diego: updated jitter range per Rahul's suggestion, merged #7003 after tightening test bounds.

ivy: merged #7002 after a tiny wording tweak. added a short stream-cancellation paragraph.

sam: opened #7004 (migration + sandbox) linking #7001/#7002/#7003. added a short "Timeouts vs Retries" FAQ and the compatibility table. requesting editorial from @ivy.

ivy: gave editorial pass, suggested a sentence tweak to avoid promising resume_token stability across incompatible model updates. updated and merged #7004.

docs-bot: (docs-bot) All PRs merged. site rebuilt and snippets page updated.

rahul: thanks all — let's watch support tickets for this page next week and add a small analytics pulse.

sam: good call. I'll track analytics and reopen if anything pops up. closing thread. :tada:
